# ── build output ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# These patterns have **no leading slash**, so they match at any depth. That
# matters in an umbrella: Mix writes its build output wherever the working
# directory was when it ran, so `_build/` appears at the repository root when
# mix runs from the root, and inside an app when mix runs from that app. A
# rooted `/_build/` only matches the first case and silently lets the second
# one into the commit.
#
# The build root can be named anything at all — `MIX_BUILD_ROOT` is set to a
# fresh directory for each run in this project — so the pattern covers the
# whole family.

# The directory Mix will write compiled artifacts to.
_build/

# Every build root, whatever it is named.
_build*/
.build_*/
_uibuild/
.uibuild/

# The directory Cargo writes Rust build output to. A dependency that ships a
# native library compiles its Rust here, at the repository root, and the
# output is hundreds of megabytes of object files that are not ours.
target/

# ── the design corpus ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `docs/` is the team's design record: the decision register, the data model,
# the security architecture, the performance budget, the Mnesia and split-brain
# work. It is working material, not a published artefact, and it is never
# committed or pushed.
#
# Publication scope: keep the entire documentation tree local, including
# architecture maps and screenshots. Only the root README and required legal
# files accompany the source, tests and configuration in this snapshot.
/docs/
*.md
!/README.md
!/TRADEMARK.md

# If you run "mix test --cover", coverage assets end up here.
cover/

# The directory Mix downloads your dependencies sources to.
deps/

# Where 3rd-party dependencies like ExDoc output generated docs.
doc/

# The assets the build toolchain produces from `apps/veritas_web/assets/`.
# The source is the `assets/` directory; this is esbuild's and Tailwind's
# output, rebuilt by `mix setup` and by `mix assets.deploy` for a release.
priv/static/assets/

# The release artefacts the packaging script writes: the tarballs, their
# checksums and the channel manifests. The release workflow builds them on the
# runner and publishes them; they are build output, not source, and a local
# packaging run must not leave one where a commit could pick it up.
dist/

# Ignore .fetch files in case you like to edit your project deps locally.
.fetch

# If the VM crashes, it generates a dump, let's ignore it too.
erl_crash.dump
*.dump

# Also ignore archive artifacts (built via "mix archive.build").
*.ez

# Temporary files, for example, from tests.
tmp/

# local SurrealDB data directories and macOS noise
.surreal*/
.DS_Store

# The managed datastore's data directory in a development checkout
# (`Veritas.Store.Surreal.Server.data_dir/0`). The store is the node's
# durable state, and it is not source.
var/

# The registry of launched processes (`mix veritas.launch` writes it). It is
# runtime state — a list of pids on this machine — and it is not source.
ops/processes.tsv

# Python bytecode caches from the CI security gate scripts
__pycache__/
.ruff_cache/
*.pyc

# ── orphaned scratch files ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A writer that stages a file under a random name and renames it into place
# leaves the staged name behind wherever the rename cannot happen. These are
# zero-byte stubs, they are referenced by nothing, and they must never reach
# a commit: an untracked directory is added wholesale, so a stub inside
# apps/ would ship without anyone reading the diff.
XX*
*_test_a.txt

# A scratch file left behind by a session whose sandbox refused to delete it.
# It is six bytes, it is referenced by nothing, and it must never reach a
# commit. Delete it by hand when the tree is next writable.
.deleteme_probe
/ln_probe/
.!*
/bench/last-run.json

# The datastore's binary, fetched and verified by `mix veritas.store.install`.
# It is a build input, not source: it is a third-party artefact, it is large,
# and it is pinned by digest rather than committed.
vendor/
rel/overlays/vendor/

# An Erlang crash dump. The BEAM writes one into its working directory when
# it dies, and a Plugin's working directory is the host's — which is the
# repository. A dump is evidence, not source: it is four megabytes of
# addresses, it is never committed, and the host points a Plugin's dumps at
# a scratch directory so it never lands here at all.
erl_crash.dump

# User added ignore filed.
.codegraph
.elixir_ls
